                    M i c r o  S   H   R   D   L   U
                           Preliminary Version


  MicroSHRDLU is a program for manipulating an onscreen color 3-D
blocks-world; it is capable of limited natural language spatial-vocabulary
"conversation."  It provides a hands-on environment for exploring some
of the prospects and limits of artificial intelligence.  You can run
"laboratory experiments" such as variations on the Turing Test for
intelligence (e.g., "What are some of the least/most intelligent
behaviors of the program?  Why?").


(Requires EGA or VGA and 512K RAM.  Runs faster when installed on hard disk.)

            MicroSHRDLU was written in Borland Turbo Pascal 5.5

______________________________________________________________________________

 To get a hardcopy of this help listing, type "PRINT MSHRDLU.HLP" from DOS.
 For a listing of the rewrite rules, type "PRINT MSHRDLU.GRA" from DOS.
______________________________________________________________________________

C O M M A N D S :


  GENERAL:
  --------

  PICK UP, GRASP       Pick up a block and hold it. [ex.- GRASP THE BIG
                         GREEN PYRAMID.]

  PUT DOWN             Drop an object that is being held. [ex. - PUT THE
                         SMALL RED CUBE DOWN. or PUT DOWN THE LARGE GREEN
                         BLOCK.]

  MOVE, PUT, STACK     Place a block on another block, on the floor,
                         or in a box. [ex. - MOVE THE BIG RED CUBE TO
                         THE SMALL GREEN CUBE. or PUT THE BIG GREEN
                         PYRAMID IN THE BOX.]

  CLEAR THE TOP OF     Clean off a block so other blocks may be stacked
                         on it.  [ex. - CLEAR THE TOP OF THE BIG RED CUBE.
                         or CLEAR A LARGE CUBE.]



  BUILD, MAKE          Runs a built-in script.  The available scripts
                         are:  TOWN, TOWER, and SLINKY. [ex. - MAKE A
                         TOWER. or BUILD A TOWN.]

  AGAIN                Repeat the last action.
                         [ex. - AGAIN.]

  EMPTY, FILL          Empty or fill the box.  [ex. - EMPTY THE BOX. or
                         FILL THE BOX.]

  WHERE                Inquire about the position of a block.  [ex. - WHERE
                         IS THE BIG RED CUBE?]

  BETWEENNESS:
  ------------

  BETWEEN              [ex. - PUT (block1) BETWEEN (block2) AND (block3)

  NEXT TO, BESIDE, NEAR

  IN FRONT OF, IN BACK OF, BEHIND, TO THE RIGHT OF, TO THE LEFT OF


  HOUSEKEEPING:
  -------------

  DOLLY [LEFT, RIGHT,  Move the "camera" left, right, up, down, or center.
    UP, DOWN, CENTER]    (Repeatable several times in each direction.)
                         [ex. - DOLLY LEFT.]
                       Or, just use arrow keys.

  CLEAR                Clears all text from the screen.
                       Or, just use DEL key.

  RESET                Restores the blocks to the positions they had at
                       the beginning of the program.

  EXIT, QUIT, BYE      Leave MicroSHRDLU and return to DOS.

  HELP                 Displays these screens.





  "MIND-READING":
  ---------------

  TRACE [ON,OFF]       Set planner tracing on or off. [ex. - TRACE ON.]
                       Block numbers used in trace mode are as follows:
                         1 = big green cube
                         2 = small green cube
                         3 = big red cube
                         4 = small red cube
                         5 = big green pyramid
                         6 = small green pyramid
                         7 = big red pyramid
                         8 = small red pyramid

  HOW                  Displays the plan steps for last action only.

  PTRACE [ON,OFF]      Parser trace mode toggle.  Displays recursive
                         descent parse of current sentence.


______________________________________________________________________________




  For information on the original SHRDLU program, see T. Winograd,
  UNDERSTANDING NATURAL LANGUAGE (1972), especially Chapter 1.

  For current information about syntactic parsing, see J. Allen,
  NATURAL LANGUAGE UNDERSTANDING (1987), Chapters 2-6.

